WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.280 --> 00:00:08.280
INSTRUCTOR: And one of
the challenges that we've

00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:11.610
seen time and time again,
is that there's often

00:00:11.610 --> 00:00:15.270
a gap between data scientists
and those stakeholders

00:00:15.270 --> 00:00:16.800
they're hoping to inform.

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:19.170
And that gap a lot of
times is the result

00:00:19.170 --> 00:00:21.130
of different mediums.

00:00:21.130 --> 00:00:22.560
So that creates
a problem for us,

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:25.110
is that if we just wait
for decision makers

00:00:25.110 --> 00:00:27.480
to proactively go
visit our work,

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:29.670
we're not actually
closing that gap.

00:00:29.670 --> 00:00:31.180
Now that's one problem.

00:00:31.180 --> 00:00:33.120
We also see something
else kind of interesting

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:39.060
happen as do the science teams
trying to close this gap.

00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:41.370
One thing as we said, is
that maybe the dashboards

00:00:41.370 --> 00:00:42.510
fall by the wayside.

00:00:42.510 --> 00:00:44.430
Another thing that
can often happen

00:00:44.430 --> 00:00:47.130
is that stakeholders
will get excited,

00:00:47.130 --> 00:00:49.500
they'll say great this
insight makes sense,

00:00:49.500 --> 00:00:54.120
can you bring it to my
medium, can you copy and paste

00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:56.250
those predictions
into my spreadsheet,

00:00:56.250 --> 00:00:59.310
or can you send me a
screenshot of that plot

00:00:59.310 --> 00:01:01.170
so I can put it in
my presentation?

00:01:01.170 --> 00:01:05.410
And that leads to a lot of
painful labor-intensive work.

00:01:05.410 --> 00:01:08.100
But I'd like to
propose for you today

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:11.190
an alternative, a
solution that we've

00:01:11.190 --> 00:01:13.995
found a lot of success using
ourselves here at RStudio.

00:01:13.995 --> 00:01:15.370
So what does the
solution entail?

00:01:15.370 --> 00:01:18.360
Instead of creating a
dashboard, what we like to do

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:21.720
is create a proactive notifier.

00:01:21.720 --> 00:01:25.950
Or something that is going to
alert me to the information

00:01:25.950 --> 00:01:27.280
that I need to know.

00:01:27.280 --> 00:01:29.340
And in our case, we
spend most of our time

00:01:29.340 --> 00:01:31.755
inside of our inboxes, so
that's where our alerts goes,

00:01:31.755 --> 00:01:32.670
is through email.

00:01:32.670 --> 00:01:34.920
And so that's what we're
going to talk about today,

00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:37.770
is how you can use a
rich set of R-packages

00:01:37.770 --> 00:01:42.060
to allow you to create these
proactive notifications.

00:01:42.060 --> 00:01:44.070
Oftentimes these
notifications need

00:01:44.070 --> 00:01:46.410
to meet the expectations
of our stakeholders

00:01:46.410 --> 00:01:48.000
for how they're
formatted, so we'll

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:51.180
look at some new packages
that are available to help you

00:01:51.180 --> 00:01:53.350
precisely format these results.

00:01:53.350 --> 00:01:56.310
And then finally, we don't want
to solve this dashboard fatigue

00:01:56.310 --> 00:01:58.920
problem by introducing
another type of fatigue, which

00:01:58.920 --> 00:02:00.900
would be email
fatigue, and so we're

00:02:00.900 --> 00:02:03.060
going to talk about
how you can only send

00:02:03.060 --> 00:02:04.770
these alerts on conditions.

00:02:04.770 --> 00:02:08.169
So that instead of
sending emails every day,

00:02:08.169 --> 00:02:10.080
it can send emails
more specifically when

00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:11.600
there's a problem at hand.

00:02:11.600 --> 00:02:13.590
We also don't want to introduce
data science fatigue so we're

00:02:13.590 --> 00:02:15.210
going to talk about
how we can achieve

00:02:15.210 --> 00:02:18.000
these proactive notifications
in an automated way.

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:19.620
The picture that
you're seeing here

00:02:19.620 --> 00:02:22.710
is one of my favorite
examples of this workflow.

00:02:22.710 --> 00:02:24.750
And one of my
roles at RStudio is

00:02:24.750 --> 00:02:26.970
to keep track of all
the different R-packages

00:02:26.970 --> 00:02:29.340
that we build and maintain.

00:02:29.340 --> 00:02:31.200
For some of you on
the line, R-packages

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:33.000
might be a newer thing.

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:35.940
So briefly they're sort of
the bread and butter of how

00:02:35.940 --> 00:02:38.340
data science gets done
in R. They're also

00:02:38.340 --> 00:02:39.812
one of the main
things that we're

00:02:39.812 --> 00:02:41.520
responsible for at
RStudio, it's creating

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:43.140
these different packages.

00:02:43.140 --> 00:02:47.700
And so every day we go and we
build all of these packages

00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:51.630
and we want to know if
any of those builds fail.

00:02:51.630 --> 00:02:54.280
Especially if it's a
really popular package.

00:02:54.280 --> 00:02:57.210
We don't want to get a
whole bunch of emails

00:02:57.210 --> 00:02:58.950
from users saying,
hey, this package

00:02:58.950 --> 00:03:00.870
isn't available or doesn't work.

00:03:00.870 --> 00:03:04.060
We want to stay ahead of
those types of problems.

00:03:04.060 --> 00:03:06.870
And so what a data scientist
named Greg and I came up with,

00:03:06.870 --> 00:03:09.000
is this workflow,
where every day

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:11.820
we run these builds and
then if there's a problem

00:03:11.820 --> 00:03:13.920
and critically, only
if there's a problem,

00:03:13.920 --> 00:03:16.410
do we get these types
of targeted emails

00:03:16.410 --> 00:03:18.540
that tell me exactly
what the problem was

00:03:18.540 --> 00:03:21.460
and allow me to then
take further action.

00:03:21.460 --> 00:03:23.610
We're not the only
ones who are using

00:03:23.610 --> 00:03:25.143
this pattern successfully.

00:03:25.143 --> 00:03:27.060
So I just want to tell
you a few quick stories

00:03:27.060 --> 00:03:30.720
before we get into the
weeds of writing the code.

00:03:30.720 --> 00:03:34.320
One of my favorite stories is
from a large infrastructure

00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:35.640
as a service provider.

00:03:35.640 --> 00:03:39.120
So a company that maintains
thousands if not hundreds

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:41.460
of thousands of servers.

00:03:41.460 --> 00:03:44.340
And what they use
this workflow to do,

00:03:44.340 --> 00:03:46.980
was track their hardware uptime.

00:03:46.980 --> 00:03:49.500
And then if they saw
a problem arising they

00:03:49.500 --> 00:03:52.530
could send a proactive
alert out to their vendors,

00:03:52.530 --> 00:03:56.490
in the context of their support
level agreement through SLA.

00:03:56.490 --> 00:03:58.440
And what this team was
really excited about,

00:03:58.440 --> 00:04:01.755
is before adopting this workflow
they had done this type of task

00:04:01.755 --> 00:04:02.940
in Jira.

00:04:02.940 --> 00:04:06.210
And any time they onboarded
a new vendor or they modified

00:04:06.210 --> 00:04:08.640
their SLA, they had
to manually click

00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:10.440
through a whole bunch
of steps in Jira

00:04:10.440 --> 00:04:12.822
to accomplish this task.

00:04:12.822 --> 00:04:14.280
The great thing
about this workflow

00:04:14.280 --> 00:04:17.279
is that it's all based on
code, so it's really easy

00:04:17.279 --> 00:04:21.329
to reuse these different
rules and different markdown

00:04:21.329 --> 00:04:23.190
documents that you're
going to write.

00:04:23.190 --> 00:04:25.860
And so this allowed the
team to easily cut down

00:04:25.860 --> 00:04:27.040
their onboarding time.

00:04:27.040 --> 00:04:30.360
Another really awesome story
of this type of technology

00:04:30.360 --> 00:04:33.630
applied, was a high tech
manufacturer of people

00:04:33.630 --> 00:04:35.910
ops department, so
kind of an HR group.

00:04:35.910 --> 00:04:37.470
And they had this
challenging task

00:04:37.470 --> 00:04:39.090
where they needed
to notify division

00:04:39.090 --> 00:04:43.110
managers about employee
overtime problems.

00:04:43.110 --> 00:04:45.930
So say an employee
worked more overtime

00:04:45.930 --> 00:04:48.530
than they were allowed, they
wanted to send out an alert.

00:04:48.530 --> 00:04:50.970
And before they had
a dashboard and what

00:04:50.970 --> 00:04:54.460
they found was their 30
different division managers,

00:04:54.460 --> 00:04:57.060
were not reliably going to
that dashboard every day

00:04:57.060 --> 00:04:59.405
to check if there was problems.

00:04:59.405 --> 00:05:00.780
And so they adopted
this workflow

00:05:00.780 --> 00:05:04.170
to instead send out
an email to a manager,

00:05:04.170 --> 00:05:06.170
only if there was
actually a problem

00:05:06.170 --> 00:05:08.930
with a specific employee's
over time submission.

00:05:08.930 --> 00:05:11.270
And they actually won
an award of excellence

00:05:11.270 --> 00:05:13.730
for adopting this
workflow, which

00:05:13.730 --> 00:05:16.940
I think is really exciting
because it's ultimately

00:05:16.940 --> 00:05:19.740
a pretty simple concept that
we're talking about here.

00:05:19.740 --> 00:05:22.190
And so if they're able
to win excellence awards

00:05:22.190 --> 00:05:24.530
I think that's going to be
accessible to a lot of teams

00:05:24.530 --> 00:05:25.030
as well.

00:05:27.650 --> 00:05:31.030
So this is our main document,
our main report in Connect.

00:05:31.030 --> 00:05:32.173
So it looks great.

00:05:32.173 --> 00:05:34.090
We actually have a history
of these documents,

00:05:34.090 --> 00:05:35.590
they'll be published every day.

00:05:35.590 --> 00:05:37.270
But to do that you
have to set it up.

00:05:37.270 --> 00:05:41.910
You actually have to go to
schedule and then schedule

00:05:41.910 --> 00:05:45.560
this output to be run every day.

00:05:45.560 --> 00:05:48.098
So it'll take all code
it'll run it everyday,

00:05:48.098 --> 00:05:50.140
maybe on different data,
that's kind of the hope.

00:05:50.140 --> 00:05:52.345
Because what happens is,
you'll be [INAUDIBLE]

00:05:52.345 --> 00:05:53.740
a different datasource.

00:05:53.740 --> 00:05:55.282
Well the same
datasource, but it will

00:05:55.282 --> 00:05:56.600
be updated every single day.

00:05:56.600 --> 00:05:59.350
So we'll get different data
and possibly different alerts.

00:05:59.350 --> 00:06:01.840
So in this day, we did
have a few problems

00:06:01.840 --> 00:06:04.490
that are lined here in red.

00:06:04.490 --> 00:06:09.540
So that's great, but we
have to check this every day

00:06:09.540 --> 00:06:12.150
to see that there's a problem.

00:06:12.150 --> 00:06:15.150
When we send email, we can
send it when there's a problem

00:06:15.150 --> 00:06:17.020
and we don't have
to be checking.

00:06:17.020 --> 00:06:19.020
This is almost the same
problem as the dashboard

00:06:19.020 --> 00:06:20.170
having to report here.

00:06:20.170 --> 00:06:22.480
But the email
solves that problem.

00:06:22.480 --> 00:06:25.050
So to schedule that, we schedule
every day and the key thing

00:06:25.050 --> 00:06:28.060
is to send an
email after update.

00:06:28.060 --> 00:06:31.100
And we set some recipients.

00:06:31.100 --> 00:06:33.500
It could be Sean,
for instance here.

00:06:37.470 --> 00:06:41.120
OK, sorry we have
to go here first

00:06:41.120 --> 00:06:47.750
and have him be as part of
the [INAUDIBLE] on the list.

00:06:47.750 --> 00:06:49.850
Sean.

00:06:49.850 --> 00:06:52.060
Great, now we can save that.

00:06:52.060 --> 00:06:54.685
And now we have a master list of
people that can view or change

00:06:54.685 --> 00:06:55.300
this document.

00:06:55.300 --> 00:06:59.680
And we can schedule Connect to
send emails to those recipients

00:06:59.680 --> 00:07:00.470
through here.

00:07:00.470 --> 00:07:02.170
So I'll just do
this one more time.

00:07:02.170 --> 00:07:03.720
This is kind of cool.

00:07:03.720 --> 00:07:06.000
Great, and we'll save this.

00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:06.500
Brilliant.

00:07:06.500 --> 00:07:08.720
So every day it's
going to be run

00:07:08.720 --> 00:07:10.222
and we can change
this of course.

00:07:10.222 --> 00:07:11.930
But this is daily
data, so it makes sense

00:07:11.930 --> 00:07:13.490
to send it every day.

00:07:13.490 --> 00:07:16.760
And it will send an email
only if the thresholds

00:07:16.760 --> 00:07:18.750
were exceeded.

00:07:18.750 --> 00:07:20.370
And we can actually
preview the email.

00:07:20.370 --> 00:07:26.590
Send one to yourself, just to
debug the email and make sure

00:07:26.590 --> 00:07:28.780
it looks good before
it actually goes out

00:07:28.780 --> 00:07:30.340
before it goes to production.

00:07:30.340 --> 00:07:33.100
So I can send this.

00:07:33.100 --> 00:07:35.080
And I actually happen
to have one open.

00:07:35.080 --> 00:07:37.210
So it shows it to me right here.

00:07:37.210 --> 00:07:40.210
This is my email client.

00:07:40.210 --> 00:07:41.680
We can see that
the subject here is

00:07:41.680 --> 00:07:44.710
one of our KPIs and
parentheses three, that's

00:07:44.710 --> 00:07:48.315
the blue work that we did here,
total exceed broke thresholds

00:07:48.315 --> 00:07:50.440
and we have the list of
things here, which is great

00:07:50.440 --> 00:07:52.840
because if you look at this
in your list of messages

00:07:52.840 --> 00:07:55.900
you see this right away before
even opening up the email.

00:07:55.900 --> 00:07:57.400
And the body looks really good.

00:07:57.400 --> 00:07:59.650
This actually looks really
wonderful on a small device

00:07:59.650 --> 00:08:01.695
because the text is quite large.

00:08:01.695 --> 00:08:03.070
We have just the
important things

00:08:03.070 --> 00:08:06.430
which are the table, a little
bit of text, just to sort

00:08:06.430 --> 00:08:09.350
of show what's being
shown and importantly,

00:08:09.350 --> 00:08:11.620
here's the attachment.

